<!-- SOC-AI - rule 100626 (level 12). Domain: threat_hunting,linux,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="threat_hunting,linux,">

  <!-- Fork bomb: process table exhaustion (ulimit -u / nproc). Detected through
       the error message emitted by the kernel/PAM/shell when the limit is hit -
       far more reliable and far less noisy than auditing clone()/fork()
       directly (millions of legitimate events per day).

       REVISION 2026-07-26: cgroup v2 signatures added. On a modern systemd, the
       limit hit first is almost never `ulimit -u` but the `TasksMax` of the unit
       or session cgroup: the kernel then emits "cgroup: fork rejected by pids
       controller" and systemd "Failed to fork off ... Resource temporarily
       unavailable". The former regex only covered the shell/PAM wording and
       therefore missed the most common case on Debian 12.

       No `if_group`/`if_sid`: depending on the case these messages arrive from
       the kernel, PAM, systemd or the shell, decoded into different groups.
       Restricting to one group would reintroduce a blind spot - the cost of one
       extra PCRE2 evaluation per log is the accepted price. -->

  <rule id="100626" level="12">
    <match type="pcre2">(?i)(fork:\s*(retry:\s*)?resource temporarily unavailable|too many processes|clone\(\) failed|fork rejected by pids controller|cgroup:\s*fork rejected|failed to fork off[^\n]{0,60}resource temporarily unavailable|can'?t fork|fork failed)</match>
    <description>Process table exhaustion (kernel/PAM/systemd/shell message) - fork bomb signature</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1499.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
